  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM MDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nAn upper-level ridge of high pressure will build across eastern\nIdaho today. While this building ridge will eliminate large-\nscale upper-level support for organized convection, deep \nmonsoonal moisture remains firmly entrenched across the region. \nDriven by diurnal surface heating, isolated to scattered \nafternoon thunderstorms will develop primarily over the higher \nterrain and mountain zones. High-resolution convective-allowing \nmodels (CAMs) strongly support this terrain-driven convective \nplacement. The primary hazards with any developing cells will be\nlocalized gusty outflow winds and brief, heavy downpours. \nConvective activity will taper off quickly this evening with the\nloss of solar heating, followed by a near-repeat setup on \nMonday afternoon.\n\nA shortwave trough is forecast to traverse the northern \nperimeter of the ridge, moving across Montana from Monday night \nthrough Tuesday. Associated height falls and glancing upper- \nlevel energy will enhance shower and thunderstorm coverage \nacross eastern Idaho during this timeframe, particularly over \nthe Central Mountains, Continental Divide, and Eastern \nHighlands.\n\nBehind the departing shortwave, upper-level flow will shift to \nthe northwest as the ridge amplifies just to our west. This \nnorthwesterly trajectory will shunt the primary monsoonal \nmoisture plume southward, pushing a drier air mass into the \nIntermountain West and significantly reducing daily thunderstorm\nchances for the second half of the week.\n\nDaytime temperatures will steadily trend upward throughout the\n7-day period. Expect afternoon highs to remain slightly below\nseasonal normals through Tuesday before surging above average\nThursday through next weekend as the ridge axis locks over the\nregion. Lower-elevation valley locations, including the Snake\nPlain and Magic Valley, will see a return to the 90s by late\nweek.",
